<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Pontotoc Creek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Pontotoc <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, signed on 20 October 1832 at Pontotoc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, ceded the remaining Chickasaw homeland east of the Mississippi to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The land was to be surveyed and sold, with the proceeds held for the nation, while the Chickasaw sought a new home in the west. They moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> from 1837, settling at first among the Choctaw.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pontotoc-creek.jpg" alt="Treaty of Pontotoc Creek" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Woolley · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chickasaw_Tribe_Mississippi_1832.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty belonged to the Indian Removal policy, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a>, Choctaw, Chickasaw and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> nations signed removal treaties in the 1830s. Earlier Chickasaw cessions in 1805, 1816 and 1818 had given up land along the Tennessee River and everything north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>'s border with Tennessee, much of it to the profit of the land speculators <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, John Coffee and James Jackson.</p><p>After the Indian Removal Act of May 1830, Chickasaw leaders met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> at Franklin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, hoping for federal protection from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, which had forbidden tribal government and extended state law over the nation. On 27 August 1830 they agreed to cede their land if a delegation found comparable land in the west. The delegation judged the land unacceptable, and the Senate never ratified the Franklin treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Squatters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> laws challenging Chickasaw self-government wore down resistance, and in 1832 the National Council agreed to negotiate with John Coffee. The preamble written by the Chickasaw minkos said the nation felt oppressed by being made subject to state laws and preferred to seek a home in the west where it could be governed by its own laws. The historian Arrell M. Gibson calls the treaty the basic Chickasaw removal document.</p><p>Landholders were to be paid for their improvements, and the Chickasaw, unlike other nations, were to pay for their own migration from the sale proceeds. Until they left, families were allotted the land they lived on as temporary homesteads, protected by the federal government from squatters, a benefit later denied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>White settlers occupied unallotted land at once, although the treaty barred them until the Chickasaw had gone. The Treaty of Washington of 1834 confirmed Pontotoc. Under the Treaty of Doaksville of 1837 the Chickasaw agreed to live in a Chickasaw District of the Choctaw Nation, with representation in its government, paying the Choctaw $530,000. From 1837, 4,914 Chickasaws and the 1,156 people they held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> emigrated there. The nation received over $3 million from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> allotments, and agents and contractors worked to defraud it. Chickasaw Removal was the least traumatic removal of any of the Five Tribes, yet it damaged tribal culture and structure for decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Pontotoc Creek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Treaty of Pontotoc Creek</b> of 1832 the Chickasaw sold their homeland and moved to Indian Territory in 1837.</blockquote><a href="/state/chickasaw-nation">Chickasaw Nation</a></li></ul></section>
